  <tr><th>Outcome</th><td>Sole arbitrator appointed; arbitration under DIAC; all pleas kept open</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>The background</h2>
<p>Radico Khaitan petitioned under Section 11 for the appointment of a sole arbitrator against Glare Glass Traders and connected respondents. The arbitration agreement pleaded was Clause 4 of the terms printed on the petitioner&rsquo;s tax invoices: disputes arising out of the transaction or invoice to be arbitrated under the 1996 Act by a sole arbitrator appointed by the petitioner in its sole discretion, with Delhi as the seat, Indian law governing, exclusive jurisdiction in Delhi courts — and a term that the buyer is deemed to accept the arbitration agreement by accepting the goods.</p>
<p>Notice issued in July 2026 could not be served. The court permitted substituted service under Order V Rule 20 CPC; the affidavit of service showed publication in Hindustan Times and Navbharat Times in October 2026, which the court held sufficient. No respondent appeared and no reply was filed.</p>

<h2>The prima facie gate</h2>
<p>Proceeding ex parte after deemed service, the court confined itself to the settled Section 11 standard:</p>
<div class="note">
<p><strong>From the order:</strong> &ldquo;the scope of enquiry of this Court is confined to examining the prima facie existence of an arbitration agreement.&rdquo;</p>
</div>
<p>On that standard, the invoice clause sufficed, and the court appointed an arbitrator. Two features of the order deserve attention for what the court did <em>not</em> do. It did not adjudicate whether an arbitration clause resting on invoice terms — with a deemed-acceptance provision — finally binds a buyer who never signed anything; that, with impleadment, arbitrability and jurisdiction, was left to the arbitral tribunal, with the court directing that the arbitrator &ldquo;shall adjudicate the disputes independently and on their own merits&rdquo;. And it did not act on the clause&rsquo;s unilateral-appointment mechanism: rather than the petitioner&rsquo;s own nominee, the court appointed an independent advocate, Mr. Vishal Singhal, to act under the rules and aegis of the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, entering upon the reference within three weeks and furnishing disclosures under Section 12(2).</p>

<h2>What the order means in practice</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>For suppliers.</strong> Invoice-printed arbitration clauses will usually clear the Section 11 gate, giving the supplier a Delhi-seated forum without a signed contract. But build the acceptance record — purchase orders, delivery acknowledgments, conduct — because the clause&rsquo;s binding force remains contestable before the tribunal.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>For buyers.</strong> Ignoring a Section 11 notice does not make it disappear; publication completes service and the appointment follows. The place to contest an invoice clause is the arbitration itself — by appearing, objecting to jurisdiction under Section 16 and preserving every plea.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>On unilateral appointments.</strong> Clauses reserving appointment to one party&rsquo;s &ldquo;sole discretion&rdquo; are, in current practice, routed into independent appointments through the court or an institution. Drafting them still invites the Section 11 detour this petitioner had to take.</div>
</div>

<h2>The wider frame</h2>
<p>A large share of trade in goods runs on invoices, not negotiated contracts, and sellers have responded by loading their invoice terms with dispute-resolution architecture. The Section 11 stage, deliberately narrowed to a prima facie look at the agreement&rsquo;s existence, lets such clauses through to arbitration while reserving the real battle over consent for the tribunal. This order shows the machinery working exactly that way — along with the quiet but consistent judicial correction of unilateral-appointment clauses, and the routine use of substituted service to prevent evasion of process from stalling commercial dispute resolution.</p>
